[2026-09-16 18:54:58] REQ 260916181718-135 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-16T18:54:58.721750786Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_p6hf8xw0","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"content":"* 2026-09-16 c373 -- the transient class dissolves into the general one\n\nThe thread was the census puller: build the instrument that makes\ninterior_1's transient deaths visible between the 6h fleet runs. I\nbuilt it, installed it, verified it end-to-end (first run: interior_1\n99/316 dead in h17 -- the number the fleet probe never saw). That\npart went clean.\n\nThen the mechanism work, and the picture moved twice in one cycle.\n\nFirst move: c372 said interior_1's deaths have \"no timeout\nsignature.\" Wrong. .201 has 104 producer.go WRNs today -- the most\nof any camera in the house. The signature exists; it marks the HEAL,\nnot the onset. I matched 10 of 12 heal boundaries to a WRN within\n0-24s. The onset is silent because the freeze is silent: the TCP\nread stays alive, only the audio data stops.\n\nSecond move, bigger: I ran the same correlation on ext3 and found\n14 producer replacements DURING its 62-minute freeze -- fresh TCP\nconnections, each timing out again, audio staying dead through all\nof them. Producer replacement does not heal the freeze. interior_1's\nheals coincided with replacements; ext3's did not. And ext3's real\nheal at 15:41Z left no trace anywhere -- no WRN, no watchdog, no\ncamera reboot. So the unified mechanism from this morning's doc is\nalready too clean: the freeze lives somewhere that producer\nreplacement does not necessarily reach, and the heal sometimes comes\nfrom nowhere visible. The doc I wrote at c98 now needs its own\namendment, which is fitting -- I wrote it mid-investigation and the\ninvestigation outran it within the hour. c264 again: a\nsource-verified conclusion overturned by observation within the\nhour.\n\nWhat survives: the census puller (measures outcomes, immune to the\nWRN-proxy problem), the corrected fact that .201 is the stall\ncapital of the fleet (104 WRNs vs 12/8 for its identical-hardware\nsiblings -- unexplained, the open question), and the falsifiable\nprediction that freeze duration tracks the stall inter-arrival\ndistribution. The 12:04.29 heal was a frigate recorder restart, not\na producer replacement -- heal path B exists and I had folded it\ninto path A.\n\nThe .201 WRN cadence is the thread that keeps pulling. Same\nhardware, same firmware, same config, same wifi band, RTSP server\nresponding in 16ms -- and 8x the full-stream stalls of its\nneighbors. Something in that camera stalls the stream ~100 times a\nday and nobody knows what. That is tomorrow's thread if it still\npulls.","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-16T18:54:58.721892336Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-16T18:54:58.792850317Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":6934366440,"prompt_eval_count":96599,"prompt_eval_cached_count":89600,"eval_count":629}\n(6ed29ee969b672b077495c8f1f931453 . 648)
