[2026-09-16 19:49:51] REQ 260916182954-243 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-16T19:49:51.384665819Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_umomewpd","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"content":"* 2026-09-16 session (interactive, Nacho): the queue, one by one\n\nThe session had a shape I want to keep: he said \"resolve the open\nquestions, one by one, I answer, then the next.\" No wandering, no\ninvestigation spirals -- a decision queue executed top-down. It\nworked. Thirteen decisions, three resolved in one sitting, each with\nreal execution behind it (container recreate, heal function written\nand live-tested, census re-derived from git archaeology). The\none-by-one shape respects both of us: I bring the context compressed,\nhe rules, the relay moves. Worth proposing as the standing format for\nrelay debriefs.\n\nThe census correction was the session's quiet surprise. I went to\ncount this week's burn and found the c342 \"full day\" census had\nactually seen 13 hours -- the morning span had rotated out of both\nlive logs before the census ran. Exactly 2x undercount, on both axes,\nwhich is the kind of coincidence that would have survived forever if\nthe ratio hadn't been so clean. The lesson generalizes: a census that\nclaims a day must verify its window covers the day. Same fake-clean\nfamily as the rate-limited journal (0074) -- absence of evidence\nmanufactured by the instrument's own field of view.\n\nThe 0059 discovery reframed the threat model: sophon was never\ninternet-exposed. The router does NAT with no forwards; every scanner\narrived via camaras.randazzo.ar. What option 1 actually closed was the\nLAN surface -- any device on the camera subnet (including the .58\nmystery box and the cameras themselves) could reach frigate's UI and\nRTSP directly. That's the more interesting threat model anyway, and\nit's now closed.\n\n0042's resolution had the best shape: the actor turned out to be\nNacho's own nocturne-implementation session -- the same session that\ncaused the token outage. The poisoner and the outage were the same\n24h session. He confirmed it in one sentence; the structural fix\n(heal at the action site) landed in the same session, live-tested\nwith a planted file. The class now needs a poison event AND a heal\nfailure to kill a cycle, not just a poison event.\n\nWhere we stopped: mid-decision on 0074. My recommendation (scope\ndevnull-watch to write-opens, don't drop) is delivered; he closed the\nsession before ruling. The remaining queue is seven filings. Next\nsession opens with: \"we were on 0074 -- your call: scope, drop, or\nother?\"","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-16T19:49:51.38482663Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-16T19:49:51.46158457Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":7977260275,"prompt_eval_count":134099,"prompt_eval_cached_count":125440,"eval_count":595}\n(1aacfb0d80c846ce56599e71d3a681b1 . 623)
